menu "Sorting Camera"

    config SORTING_CAM_FRAME_TRANSPORT
        bool "Enable TCP/UDP frame transport"
        default n
        help
            Starts the raw frame transport (TCP listener on STREAM_TRANSPORT_PORT,
            the accepted TCP client, and a UDP receive socket) in addition to the
            HTTP baseline. The baseline (status/snapshot/config on port 80, MJPEG
            on port 81, discovery on port 48888) is the only path used by the
            desktop app and the benchmark harness.

            Socket arithmetic, counting httpd's fixed 3-socket overhead per
            instance: control httpd 6+3 = 9, MJPEG httpd 2+3 = 5, discovery UDP =
            1, so the baseline already holds 15 of CONFIG_LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS=16.
            Enabling the transport adds 3 more (18 > 16) and therefore does NOT
            fit: leave this off unless a raw-transport client is being used, and
            raise CONFIG_LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS to at least 19 first.

    config SORTING_CAM_NET_BENCH
        bool "Diagnostic build: TCP throughput benchmark server on port 83"
        default n
        help
            Starts a raw TCP benchmark server on port 83 and does NOT start the
            MJPEG stream server or the frame transport (they would not fit in
            CONFIG_LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS next to it, and a clean measurement wants
            nothing else competing for the link). The control server (port 80),
            discovery and the camera are unchanged.

            A client sends one line, "BENCH <mode> <seconds> <chunk>\n", and the
            device streams data for that long: mode "int" sends a buffer in
            internal RAM, "psram" walks a 256 KiB buffer in external RAM (what a
            frame buffer read costs), "cam" sends real JPEG frames from the
            camera with an 8-byte "FRM1"+length prefix. Use benchmarks/net_bench.py
            as the client. Leave this OFF in any build that is not a diagnostic.

    config SORTING_CAM_NET_BENCH_NO_CAMERA
        bool "Bench build: do not initialise the camera"
        depends on SORTING_CAM_NET_BENCH
        default n
        help
            Skips camera_init() so the link is measured with no camera DMA and no
            PSRAM frame traffic. Modes "int" and "psram" work; mode "cam" has no
            frames to send. Turn this off to measure the link while the camera
            runs, which is the condition the real stream is in.

endmenu
